---
import InfoLayout from '../layouts/InfoLayout.astro';
import InfoSection from '../components/InfoSection.astro';
import SpecTable from '../components/SpecTable.astro';
---

<InfoLayout
  path="/printing-options/"
  title="Printing Options | Digital, Litho and Flexo | Lip Boxes"
  description="Digital, offset litho, flexo and screen printing for lip packaging, with the quantity where each becomes economical."
  h1="Printing Options"
  eyebrow="Specification"
  lead="Every mainstream press produces a commercially good cosmetic carton. What separates them is where their costs sit and which substrates they suit, and that is what should decide your choice."
  ogTitle="Printing Methods for Lip Packaging Compared"
  ogDescription="How digital, offset litho, flexo and screen printing differ on cost, colour and substrate for cosmetic cartons."
  points={['Four presses compared', 'Spot colour vs process build', 'Where ganging saves money']}
  ctaHeading="Send us your quantity and colour count"
  ctaBody="With those two figures we can tell you which press we would quote, what it would cost at your volume, and where the next breakpoint sits."
>
  <InfoSection
    id="quantity-decides"
    heading="Quantity decides the press"
    intro="Digital printing has almost no setup cost and a flat unit cost. Offset litho charges plates once per design but its unit cost falls steeply with volume. Where those two curves cross is your answer."
  >
    <p class="measure">
      For most lip packaging that crossover falls somewhere between one and three
      thousand units, moving with the colour count and the finishing. Below it,
      digital. Above it, litho. A supplier quoting both at your quantity is
      telling you where you sit on that transition, which is more useful than a
      recommendation.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <strong>Ask for two prices.</strong> Request pricing at your quantity and
      at the next volume break. If a modest increase drops the unit price
      sharply, you are near the steep part of the curve, which is explained in
      more depth in our article on
      <a href="/blog/how-packaging-quantity-affects-unit-pricing/">how quantity
      affects unit pricing</a>.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="presses"
    heading="The four presses"
    intro="Each has a substrate and a colour profile it suits, quite apart from the economics."
  >
    <SpecTable
      caption="Print processes used for lip packaging."
      columns={['Process', 'Economic at', 'Strongest on', 'Weakest on']}
      rows={[
        [
          'Digital',
          'Under roughly 2,000 units',
          'Many designs in one job, variable data, short lead times, no plate charge',
          'Unit cost at high volume; very large flat solids',
        ],
        [
          'Offset litho',
          '2,000 units upward',
          'Fine halftones, small type, widest stock range, lowest unit cost at volume',
          'Small runs, where plate cost dominates the price',
        ],
        [
          'Flexo',
          'Very high volume',
          'Uncoated kraft, simple spot-colour work, pressure-sensitive labels',
          'Fine gradients and small reverse-out type',
        ],
        [
          'Screen',
          'Any quantity, as an added pass',
          'Heavy opaque ink, thick white on dark or reflective stock',
          'Photographic detail; slow as a primary process',
        ],
      ]}
    />
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="spot-vs-process"
    heading="Spot colour or process build"
    intro="This is the decision that most affects whether your packaging looks the same next year as it does this year."
  >
    <p class="measure">
      A four-colour process build recreates a colour from cyan, magenta, yellow
      and black. It drifts within normal tolerance between runs. On a photograph
      that is invisible; on a flat brand colour or a lipstick shade swatch it is
      not, and a customer comparing a nude against the one they bought six months
      ago will see it.
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      A spot Pantone is a pre-mixed ink. It costs an extra ink station and
      reproduces identically every time. Most cosmetic cartons end up as process
      plus one or two spots: process for imagery, spots for the brand colour and
      any shade swatch. On a
      <a href="/custom-lipstick-boxes/">multi-shade lipstick carton</a> that is
      close to standard practice.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="substrates"
    heading="Printing on difficult substrates"
    intro="Three stocks behave in ways that catch designers out, and all three have the same fix."
  >
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li>
        <strong>Uncoated kraft</strong> absorbs ink into the fibre, so every
        colour reads warmer and darker, and there is no true white at all.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Metallised and holographic board</strong> reflects through
        translucent process inks, so pale colours come out metallic.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Black-through board</strong> keeps cut edges dark but gives you a
        much darker surface to print onto.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="measure">
      In each case the answer is an opaque white layer, printed first in the areas
      that need to read normally. It is an extra ink station, and it has to exist
      in the artwork as its own named layer rather than being requested at proof
      stage. The behaviour of each stock is set out on the
      <a href="/materials/">board grades page</a>.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="ganging"
    heading="Ganging: the saving most brands miss"
    intro="When several cartons share a board, an ink set and a finish, they can be printed together on one press sheet."
  >
    <p class="measure">
      Ganged designs share the plates, the make-ready and the press time. On a
      multi-SKU range this is frequently the largest single saving available, and
      it is only accessible if the specification is standardised. It is the
      practical reason to resist letting each product in a range pick its own
      board and laminate.
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      A twelve-shade launch printed as one ganged job costs substantially less
      than twelve separate orders of the same total quantity. That is how most
      <a href="/custom-lip-care-packaging/">bulk programmes across a lip range</a>
      are structured.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="proofing"
    heading="What to ask for at proof stage"
    intro="A proof is the reference your production run is measured against, so it is worth getting the right kind."
  >
    <SpecTable
      caption="Which proof type suits which job."
      columns={['Situation', 'Proof to request', 'Why']}
      rows={[
        ['Plain coated white board, process colour', 'Calibrated digital proof', 'Substrate adds nothing unpredictable'],
        ['Kraft or uncoated board', 'Printed proof on the actual stock', 'Colour shifts warmer and darker; no true white'],
        ['Metallised or holographic board', 'Printed proof on the actual stock', 'A screen proof cannot show ink over a reflective base'],
        ['Black-through board', 'Printed proof on the actual stock', 'Surface darkness changes every colour'],
        ['Spot colour central to the brand', 'Printed proof or a drawdown', 'Confirms the mixed ink against your reference'],
        ['Foil, emboss or spot UV', 'Production-standard sample', 'Position and register need physical checking'],
      ]}
    />
    <p class="note">
      Check three things on any proof: that spot colours match under daylight
      rather than office lighting, that the smallest type is legible at final
      size, and that any shade swatch reads correctly beside the actual product.
      Approve in writing, and keep the approved proof.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="what-we-need"
    heading="What we need from you to quote a press"
    intro="Four figures are enough for an accurate answer."
  >
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li>Total quantity, and the quantity per design if the job has variants.</li>
      <li>Number of designs or shades sharing the same die line.</li>
      <li>Colour count: process, spot colours, and whether opaque white is needed.</li>
      <li>Board grade and weight, or a description of how the pack is sold.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="measure">
      With those we quote the press that suits the job rather than the one we
      would prefer to run, and say plainly where the alternative would be
      cheaper. File requirements for whichever press is used are the same and are
      set out in the <a href="/artwork-guidelines/">artwork guidelines</a>.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>
</InfoLayout>
